      </example>
      <!--Q1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <figure>
            <media alt="Coordinate plane, x, negative 4 to 4 by 1, y, negative 4 to 4 by 1.  Dotted line through negative 1 comma negative 2 and 3 comma 0. The area above the line is shaded blue.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_13_06_10.svg"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
          <para>This is a graph of the inequality \(x − 2y &lt; 3\). The points \((7, 3)\) and \((7, 5)\) are both in the solution region for this inequality. Use this information to answer questions 1 – 5.</para>
          <para> Compute \(x − 2y\) for both of these points. </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>1 and -3</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Q2-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para> Which point comes closest to satisfying the equation \(x − 2y = 3\)? That is, for which \((x, y)\) pair is \(x − 2y\) closest to 3? </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((7, 3)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para> The points \((3, 2)\) and \((5, 2)\) are also in the solution region. Which of these points comes closest to satisfying the equation \(x − 2y = 3\)? </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:
      \((5, 2)\) is closer because 
      \((3, 2)=(3)-(2)(2)= -1\)  
      \((5, 2)=(5)-(2)(2)=1\) </para>
          <para>1 is closer to 3 than -1, so the point that comes closest to satisfying the equation is \((5,2)\). </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para> Find a point in the solution region that comes even closer to satisfying the equation \(x − 2y = 3\). What is the value of \(x − 2y\)? </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. 
      \(6-(2)(2)=2\) </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para> For the points \((5, 2)\) and \((7, 3)\), \(x − 2y = 1\). Find another point in the solution region for which \(x − 2y = 1\). </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. 
      \((3, 1)\) 
      Any point on the line \(y=\frac{1}{2}x−\frac{1}{2}\) will work. </para>
          <para>Use the point \((5, 3)\) to answer questions 6 and 7.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <para>Use the point \((5,3)\) to answer question 6.</para>
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para> Find the value of \(x − 2y\). </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>\(x=5\)
      \(y=3\)
      \((5)-2(3)\)
      \(5-6=-1\)</para>
          <para>The correct answer is -1</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Q7-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para> Find two other points that give the same answer. </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples. </para>
          <list>
            <item>\((3, 2)\) (from previous work), \((1, 1)\)</item>
            <item>Any point on the line \(y=\frac{1}{2}x+\frac{1}{2}\) will work. </item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
